INT. ST. ELMO'S HOSPITAL - HYPERBARIC CHAMBER - NIGHT

A steel cylinder the size of a bus, portholes glowing amber. Gauges tick. Pumps breathe. A cot is bolted to the floor inside; its occupant lies under a clear oxygen hood. The room smells of rubber and warm metal.

DR. NIAMH FENWICK, 50s, short grey hair, scrubs washed pale, sits on a stool beside the cot. She keeps one hand on a pressure dial. NURSE ILSE BRANDTNER, 30s, ponytail, tired eyes, checks a drip bag. MR. IGOR DOBROVOLSKY, 70s, the patient, thin as a rake, a diver's tattoo on his forearm, lies back with a hissing mask over his mouth.

She watches the dial. He watches the ceiling. They both know the dive was a long time ago.

DOBROVOLSKY
(muffled by mask)
Doctor. How long?

FENWICK
Forty more minutes, Mr. Dobrovolsky.

DOBROVOLSKY
You said that an hour ago.

FENWICK
I was optimistic.

He chuckles. It becomes a cough. Ilse leans in and adjusts the hood.

ILSE
Easy, Mr. D. Slow breaths.

DOBROVOLSKY
I have been breathing for seventy-two years.

ILSE
Then you should be good at it.

He closes his eyes, smiling faintly. She checks the line. Then glances at Fenwick. A look passes between them: this one is not going well.

Fenwick rises. Crosses to the porthole. Stares out at the dark corridor.

FENWICK
(low)
Ilse. His carboxy levels?

ILSE
Fourteen. Down from nineteen.

FENWICK
That's good.

ILSE
It's slow.

Fenwick rubs the bridge of her nose. She says nothing. Behind them the old man's chest rises and falls under the plastic.

DOBROVOLSKY
Doctor Fenwick?

FENWICK
Yes?

DOBROVOLSKY
You are not telling me something.

She turns. Comes back to the cot. Sits.

FENWICK
No. I'm not.

DOBROVOLSKY
Good. I like honesty. Say it.

FENWICK
The damage to your lungs is more extensive than we thought.

He nods slowly. His hand finds hers on the rail.

DOBROVOLSKY
Is it the sea?

FENWICK
It's the years of it.

DOBROVOLSKY
Ah. Then I cannot complain. She was always generous.

Ilse turns away, adjusting a monitor that doesn't need adjusting.

DOBROVOLSKY (CONT'D)
Nurse Brandtner. Do not cry in front of a Russian.

ILSE
(wiping her eye)
I'm not.

DOBROVOLSKY
You are. It is all right. We cry also. We just hide it in vodka.

Fenwick laughs. A real one, helpless, short. She presses the back of her wrist to her mouth.

FENWICK
Mr. Dobrovolsky, you are impossible.

DOBROVOLSKY
It is my best quality.

The pressure dial ticks down. A soft chime. The pumps slow. He watches the needle fall, calm, like a man watching tide.

DOBROVOLSKY (CONT'D)
When I came up the first time... nineteen seventy-nine. Off Sakhalin. The light was the color of this room. I thought: I will never see anything more beautiful.

FENWICK
And now?

DOBROVOLSKY
Now I think the same. But I am not afraid.

Ilse takes his other hand. He squeezes it.

ILSE
Do you want us to call anyone?

DOBROVOLSKY
There is a daughter in Vladivostok. She has not spoken to me in nine years.

FENWICK
We'll call her.

DOBROVOLSKY
She will not answer.

FENWICK
Then we'll leave a message.

He thinks about it. The gauges tick.

DOBROVOLSKY
Tell her... the sea says hello.

Ilse nods. Fenwick nods. The dial reaches zero.

A long hiss as the chamber decompresses. The hatch unseals. Cool air rolls in, and the light from the corridor falls across his face, pale and ordinary.

He opens his eyes. Looks at the doorway. Smiles.

DOBROVOLSKY (CONT'D)
(whispering)
There it is.
